{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1MwQgTspLDkdyvpASDd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up. The statement looks true, but I couldn't find a proof I can formalize here.\n\nThe ring is A = ℝ[X,Y]/(X²+Y²+1). Its class group is trivial because the point at infinity has degree 2, so A is a PID. The proof needs more than the obvious approaches:\n\n- **Euclidean division fails.** Take A as a free ℝ[x]-module on 1 and y, with norm a²+(1+x²)b². Dividing by a polynomial of degree d can leave a remainder whose norm degree is still 2d, so the norm does not drop.\n- **Mathlib doesn't cover it.** I listed the `RingTheory` file names (including `DedekindDomain` and `UniqueFactorizationDomain`) and saw nothing like Nagata's criterion. I did not check the Dedekind-domain or class-group files for anything applicable.\n- **A direct proof is a large project.** It would mean a Riemann–Roch-style dimension count, or a lattice-based Hermite normal form argument over ℝ[x]. I couldn't see how to finish either one here.\n\nI made no changes to `/work/fatex_010.lean`, and it still contains the original `sorry`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6701,"cache_read_input_tokens":2167,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6701,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ba305236-998d-4a75-9940-56cbd741cad0","uuid":"fffcfe3c-dc82-40c8-99f8-6925e870c865","timestamp":"2026-10-14T02:44:32.014Z","request_id":"req_011Cg1MwQMNYWbtkiQ9WWwfy"}
